  "query_preview": "..Zonal flow in place is keeping conditions dry with\nnear normal temperatures across the region. This will continue\nthrough Thursday before a wetter and cooler pattern starts to take\nshape.\n\nBy Friday a broad trough off of the PNW coast will start to dig\nsouth and eject a shortwave into the southwest. Tropical \ndepression Vance will be making landfall north of Mazatlan, Mexico\non Friday morning. Most of this moisture will move into west \nTexas, but some of the remnant moisture will be drawn up into the \nsouthwest and interact with the aforementioned shortwave. Guidance\nis split about 50/50 on exactly where this shortwave tracks. 50%\nof the guidance brings this shortwave into southern Utah with the\n75th percentile QPF totals up to 1\" across the terrain of southern\nUtah. However, the other 50% of ensemble members keep the\nshortwave to our east with much lower QPF as noted by the 25th\npercentile that keeps totals <0.2\". If this shortwave does take\nthe westerly track then it will have some decent CAPE (500-1000\nJ/kg) to work with along with steep lapse rates that will increase\nthe risk for heavy rain rates and flash flooding. The shear will\nbe limited so this will limit the strength of the storms.\n\nThere will be a relative lull in activity on Halloween as this\nshortwave ejects out and the main storm remains to our west. A few\nspotty showers will be possible, but chances are good for a dry\ntrick-or-treating window at some point on Saturday night.\n\nBy Sunday the trough will be moving into the Great Basin, and as\nit does so it will phase with the southern jet stream that will be\npulling in tropical moisture from a low near Hawaii. This will \nresult in widespread precipitation for most of the Great Basin and\nsouthwestern CONUS. There are still some timing difference on \nexactly when that low arrives. Some guidance brings it in slowly\nwith a split in precipitation between a round of precipitation\nacross northern Utah and a more distinct areas across Arizona and\nsouthern Utah. Other guidance phases the low in more seamlessly\nwith the incoming trough resulting in a broad swatch of\nprecipitation extending from the southwest up through the\nIntermountain West. These difference have resulted in a rather\nlarge spread in potential QPF amounts with the low end (25th\npercentile) generally 0.5\"-1.0\" and the high end (75th percentile)\n1.0-2.5\" areawide. \n\nA strong cold front will usher in much lower snow levels Sunday\nnight as low as 6000 feet. This will bring accumulating snow to\nmost mountain locations with 3-6 inches the most likely scenario\nand upwards of 12\"+ in areas of favored flow. Northerly flow\ncurrently looks like the most likely scenario as the trough splits\noverhead. \n\nConditions become drier with a return to near normal temperatures\nby the middle of next week as high pressure starts to build back\nin behind the exiting low.",
